From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 20:43:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: G16: optionally make resident render units
 compact

g16_resident keeps at most four render commands resident, one render
unit each. A unit's size is fixed by the qualified firmware images: the
UMA manager describes a 32 MiB pool (8192 x 4 KiB), the PB manager a
256 MiB heap (8192 x 32 KiB pages, 2048 blocks), and a pass slot has a
16 MiB TPC and an 8 MiB tilemap. All of it is backed in full: about
315 MiB per unit per VM. The extra units must sit in the one free GiB of
the client aperture (0x7080000000..0x70c0000000), at 512 MiB each.
macOS 26B123 keeps about 20 render commands resident (35 at most) at
depth 64, J604-MEASURED as a hypervisor guest with its own AGX firmware,
not the 25G83 firmware this driver runs.

Add g16_resident_compact (default 0: unchanged). Set at module load
with g16_resident, it makes resident units 2.. compact: one 64 MiB
window each, so g16_resident may reach 18 (16 compact units). A compact
unit keeps the qualified pass objects and the qualified 32 MiB UMA pool
and image, and has:
- a 1 MiB TPC and a 1 MiB tilemap. A single-layer 3024x1964 render needs
  48 KiB / 32 KiB with 32-pixel utiles, 192 KiB / 120 KiB with 16. Larger
  geometry still goes to units 0/1, since pick_pass checks the capacity.
- a 16 MiB PB heap of 128 blocks (MIN_BLOCKS is 64). Its PB managers and
  InitBM are seeded with that count: pb_manager_image() and
  PbEpoch::prepare() now take the page and block counts, and the
  qualified units pass MAX_PAGES/MAX_BLOCKS, so their bytes are the same.
A compact unit is about 51 MiB per VM; g16_resident=18 adds about
820 MiB per render VM.

Unprobed on 25G83: PB managers with fewer than 2048 blocks, render UMA
slots 35..50, PB slot and parameter ids 2..17. M4 has no PB growth
protocol in this driver (answer_growth() refuses a growth request), so
a command that outgrows a 16 MiB heap shows up as "pb: ... bad" and a
recovery. That is why it is off by default.

The lifetime ledger holds 18 owners (a host test publishes 18 and
retires them in order). The "resident:" stats line also reports compact.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
